From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 16:21:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libfetch: include 400 and 599 in HTTP_ERROR()

HTTP_ERROR() excluded both ends of its intended range, so 400 (Bad
Request) and 599 counted as neither success nor error.

http_request() only reaches the macro for an error status when the 'v'
flag is set; without it the status-line switch has already taken
"if (!verbose) goto ouch".  With 'v', a 400 or 599 instead takes the
branch commented "all other cases: we got a redirect".  No redirect is
actually followed there, since hdr_location only sets "new" for a
status matching HTTP_REDIRECT(), and the reply is still reported as an
error via the http_seterr() in the switch.  But the connection is
closed outright instead of being drained and returned to the connection
cache, and the caller's url_stat is left unfilled.

Use the conventional 4xx-5xx range.

Signed-off-by: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
---
 libfetch/http.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libfetch/http.c b/libfetch/http.c
index d57d8e8..63d8d33 100644
--- a/libfetch/http.c
+++ b/libfetch/http.c
@@ -104,7 +104,7 @@
 			    || (xyz) == HTTP_TEMP_REDIRECT \
 			    || (xyz) == HTTP_SEE_OTHER)
 
-#define HTTP_ERROR(xyz) ((xyz) > 400 && (xyz) < 599)
+#define HTTP_ERROR(xyz) ((xyz) >= 400 && (xyz) < 600)
 
 static int http_cmd(conn_t *, const char *, ...) LIBFETCH_PRINTFLIKE(2, 3);
 
